WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
[On screen: What the Viral Reel Left Out — THE TIP IS NOT THE WORKFLOW]

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.250
The viral demonstration gives us a beginning and a polished ending.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:16.227
The middle has vanished in the edit.

00:00:16.227 --> 00:00:20.205
That can prove a capability is interesting.

00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:31.000
It cannot prove that the complete workflow is ready for your account, your data, your job or your consequences.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.192
A capability is one behaviour the product can perform.

00:00:36.192 --> 00:00:47.154
A workflow is the complete path from an intended outcome through inputs, setup, action, human judgement, testing and upkeep.

00:00:47.154 --> 00:00:54.077
The missing work is not evidence that you are bad at AI.

00:00:54.077 --> 00:01:01.000
It is evidence that the demonstration did not show the operating setup.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:14.745
Our fictional team sees this claim: install one reusable AI Skill and it can handle the department's work.

00:01:14.745 --> 00:01:18.362
The useful part is real.

00:01:18.362 --> 00:01:27.766
A Skill can package instructions, examples, resources and code for a repeatable task.

00:01:27.766 --> 00:01:35.000
The leap to accountable department work is misleading as stated.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:43.667
OpenAI describes Skills as reusable workflows that can contain instructions, examples and code.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:59.667
It also says availability depends on the product and account, workspace administrators control access, and uploaded Skills still require your own review and judgement.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:07.000
That is our evidence boundary—not the reel's confident caption.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.444
Before relying on the result, fill in the missing middle.

00:02:15.444 --> 00:02:20.511
Name the outcome and actual capability.

00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:23.889
Check prerequisites and setup.

00:02:23.889 --> 00:02:28.956
Name the human judgement and boundaries.

00:02:28.956 --> 00:02:31.489
Design a test.

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:35.711
Decide who will maintain it.

00:02:35.711 --> 00:02:45.000
Eight checks turn a dazzling demonstration into something you can evaluate.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.673
Ask what must already be true.

00:02:48.673 --> 00:02:51.122
Which account and plan?

00:02:51.122 --> 00:02:53.571
Which files and data?

00:02:53.571 --> 00:02:54.796
Which permissions?

00:02:54.796 --> 00:02:57.245
Who owns the task?

00:02:57.245 --> 00:03:15.000
For our fictional community-events team, the Skill gets only approved event-copy examples and a small test folder—not the shared drive and certainly not everybody's inbox.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.447
Customization is not an invitation to paste more personal information.

00:03:22.447 --> 00:03:29.894
Supply the smallest useful context, approved instructions and representative examples.

00:03:29.894 --> 00:03:35.851
Then name the decision that remains human-owned.

00:03:35.851 --> 00:03:40.319
Our Skill may draft event listings.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:50.000
A person still confirms the facts, tone, accessibility wording and permission to publish.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.926
Do not test only the flattering example.

00:03:54.926 --> 00:04:04.074
Run one normal case, one edge case and one case that should stop.

00:04:04.074 --> 00:04:09.000
Decide what success means before the test.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:16.037
A complete public event brief should produce a usable draft.

00:04:16.037 --> 00:04:21.667
A missing accessibility detail should trigger a question.

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:28.000
A private attendee list should never enter this workflow.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.947
A working setup can go stale.

00:04:31.947 --> 00:04:39.184
Name an owner, a review trigger, a version and a rollback.

00:04:39.184 --> 00:04:49.053
Recheck when the product changes, the task changes or the team finds a new failure.

00:04:49.053 --> 00:04:53.000
Reusable does not mean permanently correct.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.560
The product and low-risk task stay the same.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.440
We change one variable: the operating setup.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:19.040
Before, the team installs the Skill, tries one favorable example and assumes the workflow works.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:35.000
After, they bound the job, limit the inputs, run three tests, name the approval and schedule the next check.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.468
Choose the next move the evidence supports.

00:05:39.468 --> 00:05:45.851
Build when the setup is supported and the test passes.

00:05:45.851 --> 00:05:52.872
Revise when the capability is useful but the workflow is weak.

00:05:52.872 --> 00:05:54.787
Investigate missing evidence.

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:58.617
Decline an unsupported or inappropriate use.

00:05:58.617 --> 00:06:05.000
Monitor a named change that could make it useful later.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.083
The same method helps with ordinary work.

00:06:09.083 --> 00:06:24.833
It already knows the project may hide four different claims: I remember it, we recorded it, this tool can access it, and this is the current source.

00:06:24.833 --> 00:06:27.167
They are not interchangeable.

00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:40.000
A useful handoff names the next job, current decisions, allowed context, open questions and the check that proves the intended context arrived.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.333
Do not throw away a useful idea because the demonstration oversold it.

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:53.333
Check the claim, build the missing middle, test a safe version and choose the next move on evidence.

00:06:53.333 --> 00:06:56.444
The viral tip is not the workflow.

00:06:56.444 --> 00:07:00.000
It can still be the beginning of one.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.000
Bring one current claim you would genuinely like to use.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Complete the Missing Middle card before you copy the workflow.
